Sorry, I wasn't as plain as I could have been.  I forgot to say that, though
the argument can be/has been made, that I don't buy it (somehow, I don't
think I'm alone, especially in _this_ group).  But, here goes an attempt 
(playing devil's advocate...)

The government as a body is charged with governing the country / province /
whatever area it's mandate covers.   (I think that's pretty solid ground so
far).  Governing involves representation of a populace in various areas
(safety, external relations are examples) by a subset of that populace,
which has been selected by that populace to do the representing (I think
I'm still on OK ground here.  By the bye, I'm obviously not an english
major <(semi-humble) grin>).  Let's look at safety.  Many areas involve
choices in order to control abuses; perhaps the major choices are
education vs. control.  Or perhaps, internal control by the individual,
vs external control by the government.  Speed limits on highways is an
example where education has obviously not resulted in self control by many
motorists, resulting in dangerous driving conditions for others.  So, in
order to protect, the speed limits are enforced by various means (photo
radar being just the most recent I know of).  I my experience on the 401
(atmittedly limited and subjective), the overall traffic speed is much closer
to the posted speed limit than a couple of months ago.  So, in this instance,
external control wins out over self control.

So, what drives people to do things which put others (let alone themselves)
into dangerous situations?  (smoking, speeding, irresponsible gun habits,
drugs, etc etc)?  The ideas must come from somewhere.  So, let's (says
the government) see if we can alter the input people get that give them
these ideas/attitudes.  So, since it was so effective in the case of
the speed enforcement, let's just keep people from seeing/hearing/reading
this 'bad influence' type material.  Besides, people seem to _want_ us to
do this.

How's that sound?  It's not nearly as tight as I'd like, but maybe has
some aspects that at first blush look pretty good.  It's pretty easy to
knock holes in, though.

There's probably other similar stuff that can be argued.  I think the important
thing for people here is the _education_ side.  If we don't know the
arguments being used, and can't effectively refute them, then we're
hamstrung by our ignorance.

'Educate, don't Legislate!'

Unfortunately, easier said than done.  horses and water and all that stuff.

