In article <3b0ksu$fs0@newshost.lanl.gov>, greeny@lanl.gov (J. S. Greenfield) writes:

>>There's nothing special about the binary groups
>>in regards to copyright violations.

> That's not quite true.  There is a substantive difference between virtually
> all of Usenet and most of the various .pictures groups--namely, the
> former have a fairly low percentage of copyright-infringing material, while
> the latter have an astoundingly high percentage of such.

I've seen many people make the "percentage" argument as to why it's "ok" that
their favorite text group has the occasional copyright infringment, but the
picture groups (and it's always the erotic picture groups where they are
concerned about the infringment, for some reason) have an "unacceptable"
amount of copyright violations.

So I guess the appropriate solution to the problem is to determine what the
"allowable" percentage of coyright violations happens to be and then be sure
that all newsgroups are within that limit.  For example, if it's "OK" that
10% of the Star Trek postings are scripts from the motion pictures then all
we need to do in the picture groups is to flood them with enough legit
pictures to obtain the required 10%! :=)  It can even be the same legit
picture posted over and over again.

As you can see, I don't buy the percentage argument.  I say that we should
hold the posters of materials accountable, not the "newsgroup".  Banning
newsgroups will not solve the copyright violation problem.  It's a problem
for all of USENET.  Even if we could get all sites to drop the pictures groups
the traffic would simply show up in groups where it had not been before, such
as misc.misc, or completely inappropriate newsgroups such as sci.math.

In fact, the only reason why banning newsgroups appears to work at some sites
is because most sites have NOT banned the groups.  When the ban becomes
universal then the traffic will flow into inappropriate areas.

> I don't think that banning this groups is a particularly viable solution
> (because I think the material will just be posted to remaining, non-picture
> newsgroups); but nonetheless, there *is* a considerable distinction
> between the picture and non-picture newsgroups in terms of copyright
> infringement.

The only distinction is in the eye of the beholder.  Posting pictures or
posting text that violates a copyright is just as "bad".  In any case, it
is up to the holder of the copyright to enforce the copyright.  Sites can
certainly help by holding users accountable.  That's what I do.  I remind
my users of the rules of use of their accounts.

Terry
