I think the most of the people at CMU fighting the newsgroup ban aren't doing so
to protect their access to digitized split-beaver
shots. They are fighting the ban because it's a matter of principle: an
administrator unilaterally decided that a certain set of newsgroups
was inappropriate for CMU's computer users, on the flimsy pretext that
the university was in legal danger. People against the ban do not think
that adults should be confined to material appropriate for children, or that
access to information should be dictated by administrative fiat.

The folks at CMU are justified in fighting hard against this ban because it
sets a bad precedent. As the student body president wrote, it's not about
sex, it's about censorship. This is a critical period in the evolution of
the Net, and decisions made now about how this information infrastructure
is to be regulated could have far-reaching effects. There are people 
out there who are eager to impose restrictions in the name of protecting
children, women, minorities, and the faint of heart. While their aims
me be noble, the means they want to use to reach those aims are not



