: if CMU had decided to censor, say, the alt hierarchy (not that I put it
: past them now -- alt.drugs, anyone?)

There's an interesting point:  The hue and cry on the net has been
"huge" in the sense that it's dominated pa.politics (where I'm reading
this from.) the academic freedom newsgroups and alt.censorship (where 
I simply looked at the headers for perspective.)  How many students were
involved in the protest?  How many people on campus thought this was a
major issue, or were seeking out information on the topic?  I saw two
articles in the Post Gazette on the issue, and heard two or three news
reports on the local TV stations.  I suggest that people on the net are
a lot more upset about this that Joe Sixpack is.
How does the net relate to the importance people put on this issue in 
"real life?"

: Well, it beats steaming rage or blind disbelief.  I used to laugh at
: universities that banned newsgroups.  "Podunk U, backwater hicks", I'd
: think, "boy, I'm glad I'm not at that university".  Now I feel like I'm
: in an episode of the Twilight Zone.

In all seriousness, does it really negatively imact your life?  I mean,
if it would, how did you manage to survive without it before you got
your net feed? You can still purchase playboy, talk sex with your 
friends, go to an art gallery to see Mapelthorpe paintings, etc.  
Before you jump on that, I'm not saying that you SHOULDN'T be able to do
that on the net, or that it neccessarily WOULDN'T be wrong to ban those
things (except the copywritten material. ;-)  All I'm saying is that the
net is another media outlet (an interactive outlet at that) and that the
regulation of this particular media is still undefined.

While the net is a powerful communications tool, it is not neccessarily exempt 
from regulation.  The form of that regulation, and the extent of that 
regulation is something we can argue over forever (and probably will, 
just like the FCC constantly rewrites the regulations on TV and Radio,
and the Supreme Court constantly has to rule on First Amendment issues)
 but I don't believe that it's "organized anarchy" as is so often cited.  
As with all other media, the common interest in protecting intellectual
property, or protecting people who may be damaged by the media,
 may have a compelling interest in "superceding" some First
Amendment rights.  (Don't bother accusing me of advocating censorship:
I'm not - the courts have said the same thing over and over again,
including the other day with a ruling on the Traci Lords child
pornography case.)

