On 3 Dec 1994, Publius wrote:

>     What do you make of this?
> 
>           THE WALL STREET JOURNAL - 2 Dec 94
> 
>           BOSTON - The Harvard Business School Faculty
>           is expected to vote tomorrow on a proposal to
>           revamp its M.B.A. curriculum, including requiring
>           incoming students to master basic writing and
>           math skills before they can begin taking classes.
> 
>    What do you make of this?  What in the world is going on?
>    This is Graduate School!
>    It is a fact that not long ago a High School diploma represented
>    a better education than what now passes for a College degree.
>    And before that, when Christian values and discipline prevailed,
>    as evidenced by the contents of "McGuffey's Fifth Eclectic 
>    Reader", the same could be said of a Grammar School education..
>    But this is the pits!
>    I suppose someone can give a rational explanation for this
>    situation. 

Believe it or not, there is an explanation, and I'm afraid it's going to 
get worse before it gets better.  In public education today, we have some 
of the most dedicated and best intentioned teachers.  Unfortunately, 
these teachers (of which I am one), have a minority of students in our 
classes which are there to learn.  These students and their needs are 
compromised by a few students who do not want to be there, do not see the 
value of an education, and have one goal in mind--to disrupt the class 
and the learning process.  Although the powers that be will tell you that 
there are alternative places for these students, and that this should not 
be an issue, in reality, the state guarantees "every" student a right to 
an education, and that includes those that don't want to learn.  
Additionally, we are seeing students in successively higher grade levels 
that cannot spell, cannot read, cannot write, and in many cases cannot 
even copy a word from notes or from the board and spell it correctly.  In 
addition, curriculum and course requirements are constantly being watered 
down in the interest of "helping students succeed," to the point at which 
a high school diploma is worth no more than the paper on which it is 
printed.  

In order to keep failure rates down, and job security intact, teachers 
are forced in many cases to "teach to THE test (in Texas--TAAS)," and let 
students retest on material until they finally get it, or memorize the test.

Before I make my next point, let me emphasize that I fully agree in the 
philosophy that ALL children deserve and need the opportunity to 
experience the world and learn in (to borrow a phrase) the least 
restrictive environment; however, what teachers are being asked to do 
now, is to modify standards in such a way that, in extreme--yet hardly 
rare--instances, that a profoundly retarded child who cannot speak, read, 
or write can sit in a classroom, and as long as he is doing what he can, 
to the best of his ability, will receive the same passing marks as any 
other child who performs the tasks, passes the tests, etc., that are 
called for in the curriculum.

I was among a large group of university students, who were disgusted and 
infuriated a few years ago, that, after having successfully passed through a 
high school program, and three or four years at the university level, were 
required to take an exam to show that we could actually form a coherent
thought, and write a well organized paper to defend a position.

Unfortunately that's the way it is.  As a result of some of the absurd 
ideas about education, many coming out of the "ME" generation, we can 
pat ourselves on the back.  We have successfully screwed up a generation, 
and most likely two or three generations.  These are lost, and there is 
nothing that can be done to take these people back to grammar school and 
start over, so we are faced with the monumental task of working 
backwards--and yes, that means that if a grad school is insane enough to 
admit students without prior evidence that they can communicate 
effectively in "STANDARD" English (No I don't buy into political 
correctness either), then they are going to have to teach them to SEE 
JANE. . . .  SEE DICK. . . . SEE SPOT BITE JANE. . . . Etc.

Hopefully, if some of the educators of today will brow beat the 
politicians enough, we can stop this mad, out of control destruction of 
the American education system.  I might add also, that it is going to 
take the voice of parents as well.  Parents who are tired of having to 
pay to send their kids to private schools to get a fair-share allocation 
of the funding, and teacher attention, and who are tired of the "average" 
child getting the short end of the stick.


