SMITTY ON A HILL:  BANNED IN U.C.
     By now, many of you have probably heard that as a result of my 
"Where's the Women?" column, I have been charged with seven different counts
 of sexual harassment.  I do believe that's a record.  Seriously, seven 
different women were so offended by a piece of satire that they filed charges
 with the Title IX office for sexual harassment here on campus.
     The free thinkers out there, a decided minority to be sure, may wonder 
what steps the University has taken  to combat my thought-crimes.  In my
official warning from the University of California, I was told that any 
"actions", like writing satire, "which produce complaints because they are 
directed at individuals, or, by virtue of their persistant pattern, create a 
hostile or intimidating climate, could result in sanctions which could range 
from administrative warning, to restriction of access, to suspension or 
dismissal from the University".  For those of you who are unfamiliar with 
"restriction of access", it's what they do to suspected rapists or stalkers,
allowing them on campus only to go to class.  Rapists and stalkers, it seems
I'm held in very high regard around here.
     Before anyone says, "What about Freedom of Speech?", I'd like to assure
you that Free Speech is a priority with the U.C. Regents.  In the letter of 
warning I received, it was made clear to me that if I had just exercised my 
right to Free Speech "responsibly", then there would have been no need for 
any of this unpleasantness.  I was also informed that if I discontinued my 
comedic endeavors, I would get off with just a warning.
     Allow me to get to the point, as I'm already in violation of the 
University's directive.  The piece in question, "Where's the Women?", was a 
joke, start to finish, the whole nine yards, etc., just a joke.  The joke was
aimed at a University community that is so uptight about issues of race, 
gender, sexuality, etc., that it has forgotten how to laugh.  The joke was 
not on, or against, women.  Now, as a humorous piece, it is either funny, or 
not funny.  This isn't the Bible,  for Christ's sake, it's just a piece of 
writing, designed to entertain you, take the edge off your day, and make you
laugh.  For the most part, those of you who read it and enjoyed it got the 
joke the way it was intended.  I did receive some scary comments from
supporters, like, "You're really funny man.  I hate those hairy lesbians on 
campus, and I hope this really pisses them off."  This guy did not get the 
joke and sounds like some kind of primate, but that's not my fault.  I'm 
guessing he was a dickhead long before he read my stuff.
     On to those of you who read it and didn't enjoy it.  You fall into two 
camps:  those who thought it wasn't very original/funny/well-written, and 
those who went completely ballistic.  Let's talk about these screwheads.  
One guy wrote me, saying it's obvious I support the world-wide genocide of 
women.  I congratulated him for finding that in the sub-text.  Here I was 
afraid that no one would be able to read between the lines and figure out 
that my true agenda is the extermination of women.  To my more literary 
detractors, it's n-e-a-n-d-e-r-t-h-a-l, as in, "You're not funny, you 
neanderthal.", and m-a-s-q-u-e-r-a-d-i-n-g, as in, "trash  masquerading as 
satire".  I received hate-mail, anonymous harassing phone calls, and an
administrative warning.  Several students were offended by the request that 
they send in nude pictures of themselves, in order to compete for the Miss 
Nude UCSC title.  I guess they'd be even more offended if I requested that 
they remove themselves from the gene pool.
     Overall, the responses I got were probably 60% favorable, 30% negative,
and 10% unamused, but coping.  However, the fact that a majority found it 
funny is not any validation of my argument.  If no one laughed, that doesn't
mean that my First Amendment rights are somehow up for discussion.  The 
people who filed charges seem to be belaboring under the misconception that 
my right to Free Speech is superceded by their right not to be offended.  
After reviewing my copy of the U.S. Constitution, I still haven't found the 
Amendment that deals with the right not to be offended.  Maybe I need a new 
copy.
     Lest anyone think I'm making light of sexual harassment, I'm not.  I 
strongly urge anyone who doesn't see the seriousness of this issue to pick 
up a copy of the UCSC handbook on sexual harassment.  It describes all kinds
of incidents that have happened here, that you wouldn't believe could occur 
at an institution of higher education.  Unfortunately, by filing charges 
against me, these women have trivialized a serious problem, doing the fight 
against sexual harassment a grave injustice.
     At issue now is your right to give and receive information.  Do you want
to attend a university that tells you what you can and can't say?  Do you 
want to live in a community that regulates what you're allowed to hear or 
read, determines which points of view are acceptable, and most insulting of 
all, decides what is and isn't funny?  To all you students out there: Fight 
Back You Bastards!  You reeled in horror at the prospect of anyone censoring
Robert Mapplethorpe's rectal probes.  I like to think my writing is more 
palatable than that, and hopefully, funnier.  Defend your right to decide for
 yourselves.  I am not a number.  I am a free man.
                                             Peace
                                             Power To The People
                                             Free Willy
                                              -Smitty
                                           bjsvec@cats.ucsc.edu

P.S. - "How many politically correct people does it take to screw in a 
        lightbulb?
        None, because they are perpetually in the fucking dark."
                                                        
